
# Task 9: Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors

## 1. Objective
The aim is to identify important nodes in a small computer network using eigenvalues and eigenvectors.

## 2. Inputs
The program uses a matrix showing connections between four nodes: A, B, C and D.

## 3. Mathematical Operations
NumPy calculates the eigenvalues and eigenvectors. The program uses the largest eigenvalue and its eigenvector to estimate the importance of each node.

## 4. Original Output
Nodes B and C have importance scores of about 0.2808. Nodes A and D have scores of about 0.2192.

## 5. Modified Input
I changed the connection between nodes A and D by changing the first row from [0., 1., 1., 0.] to [0., 1., 1., 1.].

I also changed the last row from [0., 1., 1., 0.] to [1., 1., 1., 0.].

## 6. Observation
The network connections changed, so the eigenvalue and importance scores also changed.

## 7. Interpretation
The scores help show which nodes are more important based on their connections to other nodes.

## 8. Related Real-World Problem
This method can help identify important computers or devices in a network.

## 9. Assumptions
The network has four nodes. A value of 1 means two nodes are connected, while 0 means they are not connected.

## 10. New Input Data
I added a connection between nodes A and D. The other connections remain unchanged.

## 11. Output
The program calculates new importance scores for all four nodes.

## 12. Interpretation of the Modified Problem
Adding a connection changes the network structure and can affect the importance of each node.
